##
    Writing down what a thing is, where the tooling can find it.

    An ordinary comment explains code to whoever is reading the file. Documentation
    is different: it is meant to reach someone who is not reading the file at all, but calling
    the thing from somewhere else and asking their editor what it does.

    So a comment becomes documentation only by saying so. A block sitting above a declaration
    is a remark like any other unless it opens with '@summary:', because a language that
    promoted prose to documentation purely by where it sat would end up publishing sentences
    nobody meant that way.

    Nothing here is checked for existing. A thing with no documentation is reported by nothing,
    since demanding it everywhere is how documentation stops being a help and turns into a form
    to fill in. What is checked is documentation that has come apart from what it describes.
##

global model Program

    ##
        @summary: The sum of 1|1 through 1|n.

        A blank line does not end a label, so this paragraph is still the summary. That is how
        a summary runs as long as it needs to without saying '@summary:' twice.

        @n: how many terms to add. Its own text wraps the same way, and this second line
        belongs to 'n' rather than starting anything new.
        @yields: the exact rational sum, in lowest terms.
    ##
    global fraction function Harmonic(integer n)
        fraction total = 0;

        for i = 1 to n
            total = total + 1|1 / i;
        end for

        yield total;
    end function

    ##
        @summary: Whether a whole number divides another exactly.
        @value: the number being divided.
        @by: the number to divide it by.
        @yields: true when nothing is left over.
        @throws: nothing. A divisor of zero is refused before this is reached.
    ##
    global boolean function Divides(integer value, integer by)
        yield not (by == 0) and value % by == 0;
    end function

    # @summary: A one-line comment documents just as well, where one line is enough.
    global constant integer Terms = 8;

    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine("== a documented function ==");
        Console.WriteLine("1 + 1|2 + ... + 1|8 = " + Program.Harmonic(Program.Terms));

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== and another ==");
        Console.WriteLine("4 divides 12: " + Program.Divides(12, 4));
        Console.WriteLine("5 divides 12: " + Program.Divides(12, 5));

        ##
            This block is a remark, not documentation. It opens with no label, so nothing
            reads it and nothing checks it — which is exactly what a note to a reader of this
            file should be.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("a plain remark stays a remark");
    end function

end model
